    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Thus, if we plot the differences $y_{i}$ vs. $(1-2c_{i})$ for each marker on the chromosome, the resulting $n$ points are expected to fall on a straight line passing through the origin with slope $2a=\\mu_{QQ}-\\mu_{qq}$. Figure 15.4 illustrates this point, showing two regressions using the same set of marker differences but assuming two different locations for the QTL. The regression computed using the correct position of the QTL is linear, while that assuming the incorrect position is highly nonlinear. As with Haley-Knott regressions, one slides the position of a putative QTL along the chromosome, computing a regression at each point. The regression giving the best fit (i.e., the largest $r^{2}$) corresponds to the estimate of QTL position, and the slope of that regression divided by two provides an estimate of the QTL effect, a.",
      "source_page": 473,
      "printed_page": 453,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter15:p029:b7"
